      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>mode</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>string</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Mode defines whether this health rule should be generated as an alert or a recording rule. Possible values are: <code>Alert</code> (default), <code>Recording</code>. Recording rules violations are visible in the Network Health dashboard without generating any Prometheus alert. This provides an alternative way of getting Health information for SRE and cluster admins who might find many new alerts burdensome.</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>template</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>string</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Health rule template name. Possible values are: <code>PacketDropsByKernel</code>, <code>PacketDropsByDevice</code>, <code>IPsecErrors</code>, <code>NetpolDenied</code>, <code>LatencyHighTrend</code>, <code>DNSErrors</code>, <code>DNSNxDomain</code>, <code>ExternalEgressHighTrend</code>, <code>ExternalIngressHighTrend</code>, <code>Ingress5xxErrors</code>, <code>IngressHTTPLatencyTrend</code>. Note: <code>NetObservNoFlows</code> and <code>NetObservLokiError</code> are alert-only and cannot be used as health rules. More information on health rules: https://github.com/netobserv/network-observability-operator/blob/main/docs/HealthRules.md</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>variants</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell><code>array</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>A list of variants for this template</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
